>
  <p>There's no single right number of training days. Strength coach Jordan Hoppel decides based on four things: goal, schedule, training age, and experience.</p>

  <h2>Beginners vs. advanced lifters</h2>
  <p>Beginners stay toward 3 to 4 days. More advanced people move toward 5. For most people, 3 to 5 sessions a week is enough.</p>

  <h2>Strength and size want different things</h2>
  <p>For strength, even an inexperienced person may train every day, but not with super high volume each day, because strength wants high exposure to lifting and sets. Higher frequency that you can recover from easily favors strength. Hypertrophy is more about maximally recovering from every session.</p>

  <JordanQuote>As long as someone can keep up with the schedule, recover, and continue to make gains, that's what they should do.</JordanQuote>

  <p>Jordan's test is simple: you need to be able to do the sessions well, have energy in them, and perform.</p>

  <h2>How to split 3, 4, or 5 days</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>3 days:</strong> usually full body. Upper and lower lifts on the same day, not every muscle.</li>
    <li><strong>4 or 5 days:</strong> a split such as push/pull/legs, upper/lower, or muscle group by muscle group.</li>
  </ul>

  <Media type="image" alt="Weekly training calendar comparing 3-day full body and 5-day split layouts" caption="Same lifter, two ways to lay out the week." brief="Graphic: side-by-side weekly calendars, 3-day full body vs. 5-day split." />

  <h2>3-day vs. 5-day: who wins?</h2>
  <p>Jordan doesn't pick a winner. There is no correct answer, and the person's individual situation matters more than the split.</p>

  <h2>Rest days</h2>
  <p>There is no set number. Someone training for a marathon doesn't have to take fully off days. Someone training for competition pushes through differently than someone just staying in good shape. Frequency is based on how quickly you can recover; it cannot outpace recovery if you want continued quality progress. When recovery slips, see <a href="/what-is-a-deload-week/">what a deload week is</a>.</p>
